In Vue.js, you can intercept and handle the browser's back button event by listening to the "popstate" event found on the window object. You can do this inside the created() lifecycle hook or any other method of your Vue component. Here's an example:

1. First, import the event bus in your Vue component if you are using a package like vue-event-bus:
```javascript
import EventBus from 'event-bus';
```
1. In your view, you may be auto-importing your `created()` hook like this:
```javascript
export default {
  // auto-importing created()
}
```
1. Inside the created() method, add the following code:
```javascript
created() {
  window.addEventListener('popstate', this.handlePopState);
},
```
1. Add a method called `handlePopState()`:
```javascript
handlePopState: function(event) {
  // Do something when the back button is clicked
  console.log('Back button was clicked!');

  // You can also check the state object to identify which step in your app is active
  console.log(event.state);
}
```
1. Additionally, you should clean up the event listener in the beforeDestroy() lifecycle hook:
```javascript
beforeDestroy() {
  window.removeEventListener('popstate', this.handlePopState);
},
```
The `handlePopState` function will be called whenever the browser's back button is clicked, allowing you to handle the event in your Vue.js application.

Please note that this will not work when you are using Vue CLI's `serve` for development, because `serve` runs your app in a different way than a normal development server, and popstate events don't fire in this mode. It will only work when you use Vue CLI's `serve --open` or when you host your app on a real server, like Apache or Nginx.

