Here's a sample MATLAB code for simulating and controlling a 2-DOF robot arm using feedback control. You can run the code to visualize the simulation process and the robot reaching its target position.

Please note, this is just an example and should be fine-tuned for your specific requirements.

```MATLAB
function two_dof_robot_simulation()
    clf;

    % Robot arm parameters
    L1 = 1; % Length of first link (meters)
    L2 = 1; % Length of second link (meters)

    % Target position (x, y)
    target_pos = [1.7; 0.5];

    % Initial joint angles
    theta1 = 0;
    theta2 = 0;

    % Control gains (tune for desired performance)
    Kp = 20;
    Ki = 2;

    % Initialize error integral
    int_error = [0; 0];

    % Time step and total time for the simulation
    dt = 0.01;
    t_total = 5;

    % Simulation loop
    for t = 0:dt:t_total
        % Update the robot arm
        [x, y] = forward_kinematics(theta1, theta2, L1, L2);
        draw_robot(theta1, theta2, L1, L2, target_pos);

        % Controller
        error = target_pos - [x; y];
        int_error = int_error + error * dt;
        control_input = Kp * error + Ki * int_error;

        % Update joint angles based on control input
        J_inv = inv_jacobian(theta1, theta2, L1, L2);
        d_theta = J_inv * control_input;
        theta1 = theta1 + d_theta(1) * dt;
        theta2 = theta2 + d_theta(2) * dt;

        pause(dt);
    end
end

% Forward kinematics function
function [x, y] = forward_kinematics(theta1, theta2, L1, L2)
    x = L1 * cos(theta1) + L2 * cos(theta1 + theta2);
    y = L1 * sin(theta1) + L2 * sin(theta1 + theta2);
end

% Inverse Jacobian function
function J_inv = inv_jacobian(theta1, theta2, L1, L2)
    J_inv = [L2 * sin(theta2), L1 * sin(theta1) + L2 * sin(theta1 + theta2); ...
             L2 * cos(theta2), L1 * cos(theta1) + L2 * cos(theta1 + theta2)];
end

% Draw robot arm and target position
function draw_robot(theta1, theta2, L1, L2, target_pos)
    [x1, y1] = forward_kinematics(theta1, 0, L1, L2);
    [x2, y2] = forward_kinematics(theta1, theta2, L1, L2);

    plot([0, x1, x2], [0, y1, y2], '-o', 'LineWidth', 2, 'MarkerSize', 6);
    hold on;
    scatter(target_pos(1), target_pos(2), '*', 'r');
    hold off;

    xlim([-0.2, 2.2]);
    ylim([-0.2, 2.2]);
    grid on;
    drawnow;
end
```

This code uses a simple proportional-integral (PI) controller to move the robot arm to the target position. You can adjust the control gains (Kp and Ki) to change the controller's performance.

