Step 1: Create a new React component file named `ToastContext.js`.
```javascript
import { createContext } from 'react';

const ToastContext = createContext();

export const useToastContext = () => {
  return React.useContext(ToastContext);
};

export const ToastProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [showToast, setShowToast] = React.useState(false);

  const handleClick = () => {
    setShowToast(true);
    setTimeout(() => {
      setShowToast(false);
    }, 3000);
  };

  return (
    <ToastContext.Provider value={{ showToast, handleClick }}>
      {children}
    </ToastContext.Provider>
  );
};
```
Step 2: Create a new React component file named `ToastNotification.js`.
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useToastContext } from './ToastContext';

const ToastNotification = () => {
  const { showToast, handleClick } = useToastContext();

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Show Toast</button>
      {showToast && <div className="toast">Toast notification!</div>}
    </div>
  );
};

export default ToastNotification;
```
Step 3: Include both components in your main `App.js` file.
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { ToastProvider } from './ToastContext';
import ToastNotification from './ToastNotification';

function App() {
  return (
    <ToastProvider>
      <div className="App">
        <h1>Toast Notification Example</h1>
        <ToastNotification />
      </div>
    </ToastProvider>
  );
}

export default App;
```
To sum up, we created a `ToastContext` provider to manage the toast notification state and methods. We also created a `ToastNotification` component that uses the `useToastContext` hook to access the `showToast` state and the `handleClick` method. When you click the "Show Toast" button, a toast notification will appear at the bottom of the screen. The notification disappears automatically after 3 seconds.

