In order to create a drag and drop Kanban board in React, you can use a library called `react-dnd` (React Drag and Drop). This library allows you to create complex drag-and-drop interfaces easily.

Here is a step-by-step process to create a basic drag and drop Kanban board using React and `react-dnd`.

1. Set up a new React project:
You can use Create React App to set up a new project:

```
npx create-react-app kanban-board
cd kanban-board
```

2. Install required packages: `react-dnd` and `react-dnd-html5-backend`

```bash
npm install react-dnd react-dnd-html5-backend
```

3. Create the required components: KanbanBoard, Column, and Card. You'll find them inside a `components` folder.

```bash
mkdir src/components
touch src/components/KanbanBoard.js src/components/Column.js src/components/Card.js
```

4. Implement the drag and drop functionality:

First, import the required dependencies in the `src/App.js` file:

```javascript
// Import DndProvider from react-dnd
import { DndProvider } from 'react-dnd';
import { HTML5Backend } from 'react-dnd-html5-backend';

// Import KanbanBoard component
import KanbanBoard from './components/KanbanBoard';

// Wrap the KanbanBoard component with DndProvider
function App() {
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <DndProvider backend={HTML5Backend}>
        <KanbanBoard />
      </DndProvider>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Now, create the KanbanBoard component in `src/components/KanbanBoard.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import Column from './Column';

const data = {
  columns: {
    'column-1': { id: 'column-1', title: 'To do', cards: ['card-1', 'card-2'] },
    'column-2': { id: 'column-2', title: 'In Progress', cards: ['card-3'] },
  },
  cards: {
    'card-1': { id: 'card-1', content: 'Task 1' },
    'card-2': { id: 'card-2', content: 'Task 2' },
    'card-3': { id: 'card-3', content: 'Task 3' },
  },
};

const KanbanBoard = () => {
  return (
    <div style={{ display: 'flex', justifyContent: 'space-around' }}>
      {Object.values(data.columns).map((column) => (
        <Column key={column.id} column={column} cards={column.cards.map((id) => data.cards[id])} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default KanbanBoard;
```

Now, create the Column component in `src/components/Column.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import Card from './Card';
import { useDrop } from 'react-dnd';

const Column = ({ column, cards }) => {
  // Drag and drop
  const [, drop] = useDrop({
    accept: 'card',
    drop: (item) => console.log('Dropped:', item.id, 'in', column.id),
  });

  return (
    <div ref={drop} style={{ padding: 20, minWidth: 200 }}>
      <h2>{column.title}</h2>
      {cards.map((card) => (
        <Card key={card.id} card={card} />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Column;
```

Finally, create the Card component in `src/components/Card.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useDrag } from 'react-dnd';

const Card = ({ card }) => {
  // Drag and drop
  const [{ isDragging }, drag] = useDrag({
    item: { type: 'card', id: card.id },
    collect: (monitor) => ({
      isDragging: !!monitor.isDragging(),
    }),
  });

  return (
    <div
      ref={drag}
      style={{
        opacity: isDragging ? 0.5 : 1,
        backgroundColor: 'lightgrey',
        padding: 10,
        marginBottom: 10,
      }}
    >
      {card.content}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Card;
```

Now run `npm start` and you should see a basic drag-and-drop Kanban board.

Note that, in this example, moving cards between columns won't update the underlying data structure. To persist changes, you'll need to manage the state properly, ideally, in a central state management system like `useReducer` hook, Redux, or MobX.

