Determining whether an Android device is low-end can be a bit tricky, as there's no universal definition of what constitutes a "low-end" device. However, one way to define it is by looking at the device's specifications, such as the processor, RAM, internal storage, camera resolution, screen resolution, and so on.

Here's a sample code that can be used to extract some of these specifications and compare them against a set of predefined thresholds to determine whether the device is low-end:

```java
import android.content.pm.PackageInfo;
import android.os.Build;
import android.telephony.TelephonyManager;

public class DeviceInfo {
    public static boolean isLowEnd() {
        // Get device and system information
        String[] deviceSpec = { "model", "brand", "hardware", "cpu", "cpuCores", "memoryClass", "dalvikHeapMaximumSize", "screenWidth", "screenHeight", "density" };
        Object[] deviceInfo = { Build.MODEL, Build.BRAND, Build.HARDWARE, Build.CPU, Build.CPU_ABI, Build.PARSED_FEATURES, Build.VIDEO_WIDTH, Build.DISPLAY_MEDIAN, Build.DISPLAY_DENSITY };

        try {
            // Get device package
            PackageInfo info = getApp().getPackageManager().getPackageInfo("your.app.package.name", 0);

            // Get network information
            TelephonyManager tm = (TelephonyManager) getApp().getSystemService(TelephonyManager.class);

            // Compare device information with predefined thresholds
            if ((Build.VERSION.SDK_INT < Build.VERSION_CODES.M) || (tm.getNetworkOperator() == null) || (info.requestedPermissions != null && info.requestedPermissions.size() == 0)) {
                return true;
            }

            int[] apiLevel = Build.VERSION.SDK_INT;
            String[][] cpuFeatures = Build.CPU_FEATURES.split(",");
            int[] cpuCores = {"single", "dual", "quad", "octa"}.get(Build.CPU_COUNT);
            String[] manufacturerAbbr = {"nt", "qc", "tq", "intc"}.get(Build.BRAND.startsWith("in") ? 0 : Build.BRAND.startsWith("qc") ? 1 : 2);
            double[] screenDp = {640, 720, 768, 800, 960};
            int[] memoryClass = {"32MB", "64MB", "128MB", "512MB", "1GB"};

            return (apiLevel.length < 21) || (cpuFeatures.length < 4) || (cpuCores[0] == 1) || (manufacturerAbbr[0] == "nt") || (info.requestedPermissions.size() == 0) || (screenDp[0] != (int) getApp().getResources().getDisplayMetrics().widthPixels) || (screenDp[1] != (int) getApp().getResources().getDisplayMetrics().heightPixels) || memoryClass[0].equals(Build.MEMORY_CLASS);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    static App getApp() {
        return (App)getApp().getApplicationContext();
    }
}
```

The `isLowEnd()` method in this code takes into account various device specifications and system information, such as the API level, CPU features, and manufacturer abbreviation, to determine whether the device is low-end or not. Note that this code is written as an example and might not cover all possible cases. The predefined thresholds can be adjusted based on your specific requirements or definitions of a low-end device.

